- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" generation_attr_list ")" "->" HANDLE ":" gen_type
generation_attr_list ::= generation_attr ("," generation_attr)*
generation_attr ::= attribute_symbol "=" value
gen_type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | PROPOSITION | LIST[gen_type]
-- Required generation attributes are target and specification. specification has type PROPOSITION. The generation glossary schema defines optional attributes and canonical order.` — GENERATE is a pure operation that derives a symbolic value from a symbolic specification. It may generate only values of gen_type and never REF values. The required `specification` attribute is a PROPOSITION that describes the intended artifact through glossary-defined intent and attributes; it cannot contain a natural-language prompt, text sample, quoted fragment, or free-form style note. `target` is an entity-symbol or role-symbol permitted by the generation schema. `quantity`, when permitted, is NUMBER_LITERAL and has the exact-count meaning defined by the relevant intent entry rather than a word-count over generated prose.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE arg_list? ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?
arg_list ::= "(" arg ("," arg)* ")"
arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT, which names a declared Task or Conversation by HANDLE. Task declaration names, parameters, call targets, result bindings, and arguments use glossary-defined HANDLE or attribute rules rather than unrestricted identifiers. CONTEXT execution_date is removed: all dates must already be explicit DATE literals or glossary symbols when translated. Task calls require the declared callee signature and its canonical attribute order. All other prior task scoping, return, recursion, and ordered-step rules remain unchanged, but every referenced symbol must be valid under Types & Lexicon.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- Additional bind sites: action_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; task_call_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; utterance_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION ; generation_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" gen_type` — Bind gives a typed value an opaque local HANDLE. A HANDLE is not an entity name, noun, English descriptor, or ontology symbol; it is a source-local address whose spelling is generated by literal-form. Handles may be introduced only at Bind sites, task and turn declarations, parameters, and loop bind sites. Shadowing, use before binding, and incompatible types remain static errors. REF values can be copied but never manufactured from a literal or ontology symbol.
