Previous changes: [{"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Types & Lexicon", "grammar": "type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | LIST[type] | REF[type] | PROPOSITION\nvalue ::= literal | ontology_symbol | HANDLE | list_lit | proposition | diff\nliteral ::= NUMBER_LITERAL | TRUE | FALSE | DATE_LITERAL | TIME_LITERAL | URL_LITERAL | PATH_LITERAL | NAME_LITERAL\nlist_lit ::= \"[\" value (\",\" value)* \"]\"\ndiff ::= HANDLE \"-\" NUMBER_LITERAL\nproposition ::= intent_symbol \"(\" proposition_arg_list? \")\"\nproposition_arg_list ::= proposition_arg (\",\" proposition_arg)*\nproposition_arg ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value\nontology_symbol ::= operation_symbol | entity_symbol | role_symbol | intent_symbol | attribute_value_symbol\nHANDLE ::= \"$\" [a-z] [a-z0-9_]*\nNUMBER_LITERAL ::= \"-\"? [0-9]+ (\".\" [0-9]+)?\nDATE_LITERAL ::= \"@d\" [0-9]{4} \"-\" [0-9]{2} \"-\" [0-9]{2}\nTIME_LITERAL ::= \"@t\" [0-9]{2} \":\" [0-9]{2} \":\" [0-9]{2} \"Z\"\nURL_LITERAL ::= \"@u\" [a-z0-9._~:/?#\\[\\]@!$&'()*+,;=%-]+\nPATH_LITERAL ::= \"@p\" [a-zA-Z0-9._/-]+\nNAME_LITERAL ::= \"@n\" [a-zA-Z0-9_-]+\n-- All uppercase keywords, operators, punctuation delimiters, type names, and lexical forms are members of structural-symbol; all non-structural source symbols must match exactly one glossary vocabulary category.", "semantics": "BrainCode source has no STRING type and no quoted-text literal. NUMBER, BOOL, DATE, TIME, URL, PATH, NAME, LIST[T], REF[T], and PROPOSITION are the only value types. DATE, TIME, URL, PATH, and NAME literals are atomic lexical values defined by literal-form; their internal characters are not interpreted as natural-language syntax. HANDLE is an opaque local reference created only by a binding, parameter, task declaration, turn declaration, or loop declaration; its spelling carries no descriptive meaning. An ontology symbol is valid only if the glossary category that admits it defines its meaning and type. A proposition is a symbolic intent with glossary-defined, canonically ordered attributes. Every proposition attribute must be permitted for that intent by the intent-symbol entry, and its value must belong to that attribute's glossary-defined value set or be a type permitted by that attribute.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask the manager for a two-day extension politely.", "braincode": "intent_request_extension(recipient=role_manager, duration=2, unit=value_day, tone=value_polite)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Types & Lexicon defines every legal kind of BrainCode value and every rule for producing a source token. It forbids quoted prose and makes symbolic propositions the way requests and message meaning are represented.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The former STRING and IDENT productions admitted arbitrary English phrases and arbitrary descriptive names, which made expressions impossible to trace completely through the glossary. This revision supplies a closed lexical basis, while retaining open-ended atomic data only through exact literal rules.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "structural-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Structural symbols are BrainCode's reserved keywords, operators, type names, and delimiters. They describe syntax rather than a requested-world concept.", "closed": true, "members": [{"symbol": "ENTRYPOINT", "gloss": "Declares the one execution root of a document.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "TASK", "gloss": "Begins a task declaration.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "ACTION", "gloss": "Begins an external operation.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "UTTER", "gloss": "Begins a non-effectful speech act.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "GENERATE", "gloss": "Begins a non-effectful symbolic artifact generation step.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "CONVO", "gloss": "Begins a conversation declaration.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "TURN", "gloss": "Begins a conversation turn.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "IF", "gloss": "Begins a conditional branch.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "THEN", "gloss": "Begins the true branch of a conditional.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "ELSE", "gloss": "Begins the false branch of a conditional.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "FOR", "gloss": "Begins iteration.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "EACH", "gloss": "Marks one-at-a-time iteration.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "IN", "gloss": "Connects a bound loop item to its collection.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "LET", "gloss": "Binds a value to a local handle.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "RETURN", "gloss": "Returns a task result.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "AND", "gloss": "Boolean conjunction.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "OR", "gloss": "Boolean disjunction.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "NOT", "gloss": "Boolean negation.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "TRUE", "gloss": "The true boolean literal.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "FALSE", "gloss": "The false boolean literal.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "NUMBER", "gloss": "Numeric value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "BOOL", "gloss": "Boolean value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "DATE", "gloss": "Atomic date value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "TIME", "gloss": "Atomic UTC time value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "URL", "gloss": "Atomic URL value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "PATH", "gloss": "Atomic file-path value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "NAME", "gloss": "Atomic proper-name value type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "LIST", "gloss": "Finite homogeneous collection type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "REF", "gloss": "Runtime entity-reference type.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "PROPOSITION", "gloss": "Structured symbolic meaning type.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": null, "worked_example": {"nl": "Perform an external operation only when a condition is true.", "braincode": "IF $ready == TRUE THEN { ACTION act_submit(target=entity_form) }"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Reserved syntax previously appeared only in grammar prose, not as an explicit glossary category. Listing it makes all structural tokens independently traceable.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "literal-form", "glossary_gloss": "Literal forms define the few open-ended atomic values that may appear directly in source. They are data atoms, never phrases or sentences.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "NUMBER_LITERAL", "gloss": "A base-10 signed integer or decimal number.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "DATE_LITERAL", "gloss": "A calendar date written as @dYYYY-MM-DD.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "TIME_LITERAL", "gloss": "A UTC time written as @tHH:MM:SSZ.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "URL_LITERAL", "gloss": "A URL written with the @u prefix.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "PATH_LITERAL", "gloss": "A file path written with the @p prefix.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "NAME_LITERAL", "gloss": "A single-token proper name written with the @n prefix.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "HANDLE", "gloss": "An opaque local handle written with the $ prefix.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "A literal is a member only when it matches exactly one lexical production in Types & Lexicon. A HANDLE must be introduced by a declaration or binding before use; its suffix is opaque and has no lexical meaning. No literal form permits whitespace, quotation marks, or natural-language phrase structure.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Use 10 June 2025 as a travel date.", "braincode": "ACTION act_search_travel(origin=entity_tel_aviv, destination=entity_berlin, date=@d2025-06-10)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Exact literal rules allow necessary open values such as dates and paths without reintroducing unrestricted text.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "operation-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Operation symbols name the external operations an Action may perform. Each operation entry defines its required attributes, optional attributes, accepted value categories, result type, and result identity behavior.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "act_send_email", "gloss": "Send a structured message through email.", "nl_synonyms": ["email", "send email"]}, {"symbol": "act_search_travel", "gloss": "Search for travel options using symbolic travel criteria.", "nl_synonyms": ["search flights", "find flights"]}, {"symbol": "act_rinse", "gloss": "Rinse a physical entity at a destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["rinse", "wash"]}, {"symbol": "act_place", "gloss": "Place a referenced entity at a destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["put", "place"]}, {"symbol": "act_check_replied", "gloss": "Query whether a recipient has replied by a deadline.", "nl_synonyms": ["check reply", "has replied"]}], "membership_rule": "An operation symbol has exact form act_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary contains an operation entry with that symbol, one canonical signature, one result type, and one result-identity classification. A source operation may not use an unregistered act_ token.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse the mug in the sink.", "braincode": "ACTION act_rinse(target=entity_mug, destination=entity_sink) -> $mug : REF[PROPOSITION]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Replacing Action's unrestricted IDENT operation slot is necessary to make operation names and their signatures glossary-defined.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "entity-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Entity symbols name objects, places, systems, documents, and domain concepts used as subjects or destinations. Their meaning comes from the glossary, not from English word formation.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "entity_mug", "gloss": "A mug.", "nl_synonyms": ["mug", "cup"]}, {"symbol": "entity_sink", "gloss": "A sink.", "nl_synonyms": ["sink"]}, {"symbol": "entity_coffee_maker", "gloss": "A coffee-making appliance.", "nl_synonyms": ["coffee maker", "coffee machine"]}, {"symbol": "entity_tel_aviv", "gloss": "Tel Aviv.", "nl_synonyms": ["Tel Aviv"]}, {"symbol": "entity_berlin", "gloss": "Berlin.", "nl_synonyms": ["Berlin"]}], "membership_rule": "An entity symbol has exact form entity_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary contains a corresponding entity entry with a single canonical meaning and type classification.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put the mug in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ACTION act_place(target=$mug, destination=entity_coffee_maker)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Entities formerly appeared as arbitrary strings. A dedicated ontology category gives them canonical symbolic forms and synonym mappings.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "role-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Role symbols name recipients, participants, and other relational positions. They distinguish a role from an arbitrary proper name or entity.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "role_manager", "gloss": "The manager relevant to the request context.", "nl_synonyms": ["manager", "my manager"]}, {"symbol": "role_user", "gloss": "The human user in the current interaction.", "nl_synonyms": ["user", "me"]}, {"symbol": "role_agent", "gloss": "The agent executing or responding to the request.", "nl_synonyms": ["agent", "assistant"]}], "membership_rule": "A role symbol has exact form role_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary defines its relational meaning and permitted use sites.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Send the manager a message.", "braincode": "ACTION act_send_email(recipient=role_manager, content=intent_follow_up())"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Recipients were previously arbitrary strings. Separating role symbols from entities supports canonical recipient encoding.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "intent-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Intent symbols name the meaning of a structured request, message, or generated artifact. Each intent entry lists its allowed attributes and the allowed value category for each attribute.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "intent_request_extension", "gloss": "Ask a recipient to extend a deadline or allowed period.", "nl_synonyms": ["ask for an extension", "request more time"]}, {"symbol": "intent_follow_up", "gloss": "Send a reminder or subsequent request about an earlier matter.", "nl_synonyms": ["follow up", "send a reminder"]}, {"symbol": "intent_acknowledge", "gloss": "Acknowledge a prior statement or action.", "nl_synonyms": ["acknowledge", "say thanks"]}, {"symbol": "intent_explain", "gloss": "Request or provide an explanation of a symbolic topic.", "nl_synonyms": ["explain", "tell me about"]}], "membership_rule": "An intent symbol has exact form intent_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary defines its canonical meaning, required attributes, optional attributes, attribute order, and allowed value categories.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask for a two-day extension.", "braincode": "intent_request_extension(duration=2, unit=value_day)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Intent symbols replace prose content with inspectable structured meaning while allowing the glossary to grow without changing grammar.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "attribute-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Attribute symbols label the named parts of an operation or proposition. Each attribute entry defines where it may occur, its value type or category, and its canonical ordering position.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "target", "gloss": "The entity or subject an operation acts on.", "nl_synonyms": ["target", "item"]}, {"symbol": "recipient", "gloss": "The intended receiver of a communication or request.", "nl_synonyms": ["recipient", "to"]}, {"symbol": "content", "gloss": "A required PROPOSITION expressing symbolic message or artifact meaning.", "nl_synonyms": ["message", "content"]}, {"symbol": "duration", "gloss": "A numeric amount of time or length.", "nl_synonyms": ["duration", "length"]}, {"symbol": "unit", "gloss": "The unit associated with a quantity.", "nl_synonyms": ["unit"]}, {"symbol": "tone", "gloss": "A controlled communicative tone value.", "nl_synonyms": ["tone", "style"]}, {"symbol": "deadline", "gloss": "A DATE or TIME by which a condition is checked.", "nl_synonyms": ["deadline", "by"]}], "membership_rule": "An attribute symbol has exact form [a-z][a-z0-9_]* and is valid only when the complete glossary contains an attribute entry specifying its allowed parent symbols, accepted types or vocabulary categories, and canonical position. Attribute spelling alone does not create an attribute.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Request an extension for two days politely.", "braincode": "intent_request_extension(duration=2, unit=value_day, tone=value_polite)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "The prior generic IDENT=value attribute production admitted undefined attribute names and values. This category makes both the labels and their schemas inspectable.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "attribute-value-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Attribute-value symbols are controlled values used by glossary-defined attributes. They provide canonical forms for values such as tone, time units, ordering, and format.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "value_polite", "gloss": "A courteous and respectful tone.", "nl_synonyms": ["polite", "politely", "courteously"]}, {"symbol": "value_formal", "gloss": "A formal tone.", "nl_synonyms": ["formal", "formally"]}, {"symbol": "value_neutral", "gloss": "A neutral tone.", "nl_synonyms": ["neutral", "neutrally"]}, {"symbol": "value_day", "gloss": "The calendar-day unit.", "nl_synonyms": ["day", "days"]}, {"symbol": "value_price_ascending", "gloss": "Order values from lower price to higher price.", "nl_synonyms": ["cheapest first", "price ascending"]}], "membership_rule": "An attribute-value symbol has exact form value_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary defines it and lists every attribute for which it is allowed. A value may not be inferred from an English adjective or phrase.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Write politely.", "braincode": "UTTER intent_acknowledge(tone=value_polite)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Controlled values eliminate formerly free tone, register, ordering, metric, and filter strings while preserving distinctions through a growing ontology.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= ACTION operation_symbol \"(\" action_attr_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?\naction_attr_list ::= action_attr (\",\" action_attr)*\naction_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value\n-- The operation-symbol glossary entry supplies the complete ordered signature for each operation. An action may contain exactly the attributes permitted by that signature, in that entry's order.", "semantics": "Action is the only construct that interacts with external state. Its operation must be an operation-symbol whose glossary entry defines an exact signature, accepted categories or types, result type, and identity classification. No default operation signature exists. Every attribute label and every value is validated against that operation's glossary signature; duplicate, omitted-required, extra, unordered, or type-incompatible attributes are static errors. `content`, when allowed, has type PROPOSITION and never accepts prose, a STRING, or a register note. An Action result may bind only to a HANDLE and only with the exact result type declared by the operation entry.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Send the manager a polite request for a two-day extension.", "braincode": "ACTION act_send_email(recipient=role_manager, content=intent_request_extension(duration=2, unit=value_day, tone=value_polite))"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs one glossary-defined external operation using only glossary-defined attributes and values. Its message meaning is a symbolic proposition rather than a quoted instruction.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Action contained the largest concentration of free IDENT, STRING, content, metric, ordering, and filter slots. Operation signatures now live in the operation glossary and all payload meaning is structurally represented.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol \"(\" utterance_attr_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION)?\nutterance_attr_list ::= utterance_attr (\",\" utterance_attr)*\nutterance_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value\n-- The intent-symbol glossary entry defines required and optional utterance attributes and their canonical order.", "semantics": "UTTER records a non-effectful speech act whose substantive meaning is the intent symbol and its structured attributes. It is legal only inside a CONVO TURN body. An utterance may use only attributes authorized by its intent-symbol entry, and each value must meet that attribute's glossary-defined category or type rule. Tone, recipient, topic, quantity, correction, and all other conversational content are symbolic. There is no content string, register_note, or prose fallback. A result binding, when present, binds the complete PROPOSITION to a HANDLE for later use within the same Turn scope.", "worked_example": {"nl": "The user politely asks the manager for a two-day extension.", "braincode": "CONVO $extension_dialogue { TURN $t1 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER intent_request_extension(recipient=role_manager, duration=2, unit=value_day, tone=value_polite) } }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records a symbolic speech act in a conversation without causing an external effect. What was said is represented by a glossary-defined intent and attributes, not by quoted prose.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Utterance explicitly preserved natural-language content and free-form register notes, directly violating N1 and N4. Structured intent propositions retain conversational richness without hidden prose.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= GENERATE \"(\" generation_attr_list \")\" \"->\" HANDLE \":\" gen_type\ngeneration_attr_list ::= generation_attr (\",\" generation_attr)*\ngeneration_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | PROPOSITION | LIST[gen_type]\n-- Required generation attributes are target and specification. specification has type PROPOSITION. The generation glossary schema defines optional attributes and canonical order.", "semantics": "GENERATE is a pure operation that derives a symbolic value from a symbolic specification. It may generate only values of gen_type and never REF values. The required `specification` attribute is a PROPOSITION that describes the intended artifact through glossary-defined intent and attributes; it cannot contain a natural-language prompt, text sample, quoted fragment, or free-form style note. `target` is an entity-symbol or role-symbol permitted by the generation schema. `quantity`, when permitted, is NUMBER_LITERAL and has the exact-count meaning defined by the relevant intent entry rather than a word-count over generated prose.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Generate a concise explanation of taxes for the user.", "braincode": "GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_explain(topic=entity_tax, format=value_concise)) -> $response : PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate creates a symbolic value from a symbolic specification. It does not accept an English prompt or produce an unstructured text payload in BrainCode source.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Generate formerly depended on free-text instruction and output strings. Its specification is now an inspectable proposition, so generated-request meaning remains inside the formal language.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (\":\" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= HANDLE \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= RETURN value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation\ntask_call ::= HANDLE arg_list? (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value", "semantics": "A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT, which names a declared Task or Conversation by HANDLE. Task declaration names, parameters, call targets, result bindings, and arguments use glossary-defined HANDLE or attribute rules rather than unrestricted identifiers. CONTEXT execution_date is removed: all dates must already be explicit DATE literals or glossary symbols when translated. Task calls require the declared callee signature and its canonical attribute order. All other prior task scoping, return, recursion, and ordered-step rules remain unchanged, but every referenced symbol must be valid under Types & Lexicon.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Search travel from Tel Aviv to Berlin on 10 June 2025.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $book_flight\nTASK $book_flight : LIST[REF[PROPOSITION]] {\n  ACTION act_search_travel(origin=entity_tel_aviv, destination=entity_berlin, date=@d2025-06-10) -> $flights : LIST[REF[PROPOSITION]]\n  RETURN $flights\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is an ordered named procedure using opaque local handles and fully symbolic values. It contains no translation-time prose context or unconstrained identifier names.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Task's unconstrained declaration identifiers, argument labels, and quoted execution-date context violated complete token traceability. Handles and DATE literals give those roles defined lexical forms.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= LET HANDLE \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional bind sites: action_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" type ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION ; generation_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" gen_type", "semantics": "Bind gives a typed value an opaque local HANDLE. A HANDLE is not an entity name, noun, English descriptor, or ontology symbol; it is a source-local address whose spelling is generated by literal-form. Handles may be introduced only at Bind sites, task and turn declarations, parameters, and loop bind sites. Shadowing, use before binding, and incompatible types remain static errors. REF values can be copied but never manufactured from a literal or ontology symbol.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Remember the symbolic request for later use.", "braincode": "LET $request : PROPOSITION = intent_request_extension(duration=2, unit=value_day)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind stores a typed symbolic value under an opaque local handle. The handle is only a formal reference and does not hide descriptive meaning in an English variable name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The prior naming convention required English-derived identifiers such as mug and therefore made identifier semantics dependent on natural language. Opaque handles separate local reference from ontology meaning.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER \"=\" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT", "semantics": "Conversation is an ordered collection of turns identified by opaque HANDLEs. USER and AGENT are structural-symbol members. REPLY_TO and REVISES target already declared Turn handles in the same Conversation. Turns retain the existing independent scope, adjacency, and linear whole-body revision semantics; however, every Utterance in every Turn is now symbolic under Utterance, so conversational substance is not carried in prose. A Turn may contain only steps whose values satisfy Types & Lexicon.", "worked_example": {"nl": "The user asks for an extension, then corrects it to three days.", "braincode": "CONVO $extension_dialogue {\n  TURN $t1 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER intent_request_extension(recipient=role_manager, duration=2, unit=value_day)\n  }\n  TURN $t2 SPEAKER=USER REVISES $t1 {\n    UTTER intent_request_extension(recipient=role_manager, duration=3, unit=value_day)\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records ordered symbolic turns and explicit corrections. Turn names are opaque handles, and utterance meaning is formal structure rather than quoted dialogue.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Conversation examples and turn identifiers previously embedded natural-language dialogue and free identifiers. The same correction model can operate unchanged over symbolic utterances and handles.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Flow-If", "grammar": "flow_if ::= IF check THEN \"{\" step+ \"}\" (ELSE \"{\" step+ \"}\")?", "semantics": "Conditional branching retains its existing evaluation, scope, and ordering rules. Every value used by its Check must be a glossary-valid value under Types & Lexicon; in particular, no check may compare or test a quoted string or undefined identifier. Deferred external checks must use a TIME_LITERAL, DATE_LITERAL, or glossary-defined temporal value accepted by their operation signature.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If the manager has not replied by 6 June 2025, send a polite follow-up.", "braincode": "TASK $follow_up {\n  ACTION act_check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=@d2025-06-06) -> $replied : BOOL\n  IF NOT $replied THEN {\n    ACTION act_send_email(recipient=role_manager, content=intent_follow_up(tone=value_polite))\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If chooses a symbolic branch using a typed formal check. It cannot test hidden text or use an undefined value.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The construct's grammar remains useful, but its prior example and deadline semantics relied on quoted strings. This revision aligns all checked values with the closed lexical system.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Iterate", "grammar": "flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable \"{\" step+ \"}\"\niterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit\nquantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check", "semantics": "FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain their existing finite-list, sequential evaluation, scope, and no-shadowing behavior. Loop and quantifier variables are HANDLEs, so they are opaque formal bindings rather than English-derived names. List elements must be glossary-valid values of one homogeneous static type.", "worked_example": {"nl": "For each city in the itinerary, search travel options.", "braincode": "LET $itinerary : LIST[PROPOSITION] = [intent_location(place=entity_paris), intent_location(place=entity_lyon)]\nFOR EACH $stop IN $itinerary {\n  ACTION act_search_travel(destination=$stop)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Iterate processes each member of a formal finite list using an opaque loop handle. It does not introduce unlisted descriptive words through variable names or string lists.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Loop identifiers and prior string-list examples were unconstrained. Handle binding and symbolic list values make iteration conform to the closed vocabulary requirement.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Check", "grammar": "check ::= disjunction\ndisjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*\nconjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*\nnegation ::= NOT negation | primary\nprimary ::= \"(\" check \")\" | quantifier | comparison\ncomparison ::= value (comp_op value)?\ncomp_op ::= \"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \">\" | \">=\"", "semantics": "Check remains a pure typed boolean expression with the established precedence, short-circuiting, truthiness, and comparison rules. Its operands are now restricted to Types & Lexicon values: atomic literals, glossary symbols, handles, lists, and propositions. PROPOSITION supports only equality and inequality, comparing the intent symbol and canonically ordered attribute sequence recursively. No free string, quoted phrase, or undeclared identifier can be an operand.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Proceed only if the total exceeds 50 and membership is true.", "braincode": "LET $cart_total : NUMBER = 65\nLET $is_member : BOOL = TRUE\n$cart_total > 50 AND $is_member == TRUE"}, "glossary_gloss": "Check combines typed formal values into a true-or-false condition. Its operands must be glossary-defined symbols, exact literals, or previously bound handles.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Check inherited free STRING and IDENT values from Types & Lexicon. Updating its semantic domain ensures a condition cannot conceal undefined lexical material.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}]
Critic's decision: needs-rework
Critic's reasoning: The central idea—replace free text with typed literals, glossary categories, and recursive propositions—is a positive direction and removes the prior direct prose violations. It cannot be accepted because N1 remains hard-violated by an incomplete structural vocabulary, and the Task revision makes all UTTER-containing conversations grammatically invalid. The proposal also leaves deterministic handle allocation and canonical plan selection unresolved and lacks a symbolic code representation needed for sampled closed SWE-bench tasks.
Required changes (address every one explicitly): [{"target": "structural-symbol", "change": "Make structural-symbol genuinely complete: individually list, or give an exact finite lexical membership rule covering, every keyword, operator, delimiter, and type token usable in source. At minimum cover SPEAKER, USER, AGENT, REPLY_TO, REVISES, DECREASES, ANY, ALL, SATISFIES, ==, !=, <, <=, >, >=, ->, =, :, parentheses, braces, brackets, commas, and the lexical delimiters/prefixes used by literals. Add their plain-language meanings and a worked example that contains the full set of source-level token classes."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Restore utterance to step (or define distinct task_step and turn_step productions) so a CONVO TURN can contain UTTER as required by Utterance and Conversation. Revalidate and correct every Conversation and Utterance worked example after this change."}, {"target": "Types & Lexicon", "change": "Define one canonical opaque-handle allocation rule, such as preorder introduction order $v1, $v2, ..., and require it for entrypoints, tasks, turns, parameters, results, and loop variables. The current HANDLE regex accepts arbitrary semantically suggestive spellings and prevents deterministic encoding."}, {"target": "operation-symbol and intent-symbol", "change": "Add a canonical construct-selection/planning rule that determines when a request is encoded as TASK/GENERATE versus CONVO/UTTER and, for common closed task patterns, determines canonical decomposition rather than allowing equivalent find-and-loop and repeated-action plans."}, {"target": "literal-form", "change": "Add a non-prose, recursively structured CODE/PROGRAM category sufficient to encode source-language tokens, calls, assignment, lists, expected/actual outputs, configuration mappings, commands, and assertions. Define typed symbolic syntax-tree constructs and vocabulary categories rather than a quoted code string; this is required to cover SWE-bench without violating N4."}, {"target": "Types & Lexicon", "change": "Define valid literal lexical forms completely: specify calendar validity for DATE_LITERAL, ranges for TIME_LITERAL, URL grammar/boundaries, PATH normalization, NAME normalization/casing, and whether lexical productions are mutually exclusive. Also define the static type of ontology symbols and constrain ontology_symbol so an operation symbol cannot be passed wherever an arbitrary value is accepted."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Replace the removal of CONTEXT with a glossary-defined, typed translation-anchor mechanism for resolving relative dates, or require relative dates to be rejected unless a DATE/TIME anchor is supplied. The current proposal cannot deterministically translate 'Friday' while preserving auditable source meaning."}]
Logic issues: ["N1 is not realized: structural-symbol is declared closed but omits numerous source tokens used by grammar and examples, and its null membership_rule does not define the omitted tokens.", "Conversation is broken because revised Task.step excludes utterance, while turn ::= ... { step+ } and Utterance requires legal placement inside a CONVO TURN body.", "The Conversation worked examples use SPEAKER and USER, neither of which is a listed structural-symbol member.", "The claim that every ontology symbol matches exactly one category is not enforced by the grammar: ontology_symbol alternatives and attribute-symbol's unrestricted lowercase lexical form can overlap without a global prefix/reserved-word disjointness rule.", "HANDLE suffixes are claimed opaque but remain arbitrary source tokens; absent a canonical allocation rule, equivalent expressions can differ in every binding name.", "The proposal asserts canonical attribute order but supplies no actual schemas for the shown operation and intent members; the examples depend on unshown signatures.", "The Types & Lexicon worked example uses recipient, duration, unit, and tone but does not establish their allowed parent/schema rules in the supplied entries.", "No representational category exists for program/configuration syntax and exact multi-token outputs, producing a genuine closed-domain coverage failure for SWE-bench."]
Pre-KPI assessment: {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The ontology-category approach can express many closed operational tasks assuming N6's complete glossary, including household placement, shopping constraints, and structured communication. However, closed SWE-bench requests containing source code, exact expected/actual program output, configuration fragments, and reproduction procedures have no literal or symbolic code-data category, so their actionable core cannot be fully encoded."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Recursive PROPOSITION values materially improve representation of intent, quantities, recipients, tone, corrections, and nested constraints without prose. But code-bearing items fail, and the revised Task grammar accidentally removes utterance from step even though Conversation requires UTTER in a Turn body; consequently the supplied Conversation worked examples are not valid expressions."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "The change removes free prose and gives canonical attribute ordering only in unspecified future glossary entries, which is directionally useful. Nevertheless HANDLE spellings remain arbitrary ($book_flight, $main, $t1, $request), operation/intention selection is open-ended, and independent translations visibly diverge between distinct plans such as repeated pick/place versus find-and-loop and TASK/GENERATE versus CONVO/UTTER; no canonical strategy or handle-allocation rule resolves those alternatives."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Typed propositions and explicit ontology categories are easier to inspect than quoted content. But the claimed closed structural vocabulary is incomplete: expressions use or grammar requires SPEAKER, USER, AGENT, REPLY_TO, REVISES, DECREASES, ANY, ALL, SATISFIES, comparison operators, arrows, assignment, braces, brackets, commas, and delimiters that are not individually listed or generated by a precise glossary membership rule; a reader cannot look them all up in the supplied glossary."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Structured operation signatures, pure checks, typed bindings, and symbolic message propositions would help a weaker model plan ordinary operational tasks. The invalid conversation grammar and inability to encode source-code evidence are severe practical gaps, and unconstrained opaque-handle naming adds ceremony without preserving useful information."}}
Simulated examples that exposed gaps: [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_find(target=entity_pillow, source=entity_sofa, quantity=2) -> $v2 : LIST[REF[NAME]]\n  FOR EACH $v3 IN $v2 {\n    ACTION act_place(target=$v3, destination=entity_armchair)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "operation-symbol, entity-symbol, attribute-symbol, and typed handles enable the operational core; act_find, source, quantity, entity_pillow, entity_sofa, and entity_armchair are assumed members of the complete existing categories under N6."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_check_replied(target=role_manager, deadline=@d2026-10-02) -> $v2 : BOOL\n  IF NOT $v2 THEN {\n    ACTION act_send_email(recipient=role_manager, content=intent_follow_up(tone=value_polite))\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Symbolic content and DATE_LITERAL avoid prose. The date necessarily depends on an unstated translation anchor after removal of CONTEXT, so the proposed language has no way to audit which Friday was intended."}, {"item_id": "e344b89e-767a-4618-ba82-4b81cdcba280", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor at any price to the shopping cart.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_search_product(target=entity_motherboard, filter=intent_price(limit=200, relation=value_less_than)) -> $v2 : LIST[REF[PROPOSITION]]\n  ACTION act_select_compatible(target=$v2, related=entity_processor) -> $v3 : LIST[REF[PROPOSITION]]\n  ACTION act_add_to_cart(target=[$v2, $v3])\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The proposed category architecture can cover the constraints under N6, but it needs exact operation signatures for selection, compatibility, list combination, and cart addition."}, {"item_id": "f118238f-ef8f-4b63-9159-a81e981ef46e", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Add decorative LED Candles to the cart.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_search_product(target=entity_decorative_led_candles) -> $v2 : LIST[REF[PROPOSITION]]\n  ACTION act_add_to_cart(target=$v2)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Uses assumed complete operation and entity entries; no prose fallback is needed."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_214148_552057#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "get a cup ready for coffee", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_pick_up(target=entity_cup, source=entity_fridge) -> $v2 : REF[NAME]\n  ACTION act_heat(target=$v2, destination=entity_microwave) -> $v3 : REF[NAME]\n  ACTION act_place(target=$v3, destination=entity_coffee_maker)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The trajectory disambiguates 'ready for coffee' as retrieving, heating, and positioning the cup. Current listed operations do not include act_pick_up, act_heat, or these entities, but N6 permits members of the established categories."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_102045_139402#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "To put the toilet paper away.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION act_pick_up(target=entity_toilet_paper, source=entity_toilet) -> $v2 : REF[NAME]\n  ACTION act_place(target=$v2, destination=entity_wall_holder)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The closed action core is expressible through assumed operation and entity entries."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-15345", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "mathematica_code gives wrong output with Max\nIf I run the code\r\n\r\n```\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\nmathematica_code(Max(x,2))\r\n```\r\n\r\nthen I would expect the output `'Max[x,2]'` which is valid Mathematica code but instead I get `'Max(2, x)'` which is not valid Mathematica code.\n", "braincode": "FAIL: no BrainCode expression exists under the proposed grammar.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "There is no glossary-defined atomic CODE literal, token-sequence construct, syntax-tree vocabulary, or proposition schema capable of preserving the executable Python snippet and its exact expected and actual Mathematica outputs. NAME_LITERAL cannot hold these multi-token fragments."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15098", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Internationalisation didn't support language locale containing both script and region.", "braincode": "FAIL: no BrainCode expression exists under the proposed grammar.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The actionable request depends on precise settings.py and urls.py code/configuration, BCP-47 tags, URLs, observed HTTP results, expected results, and reproduction commands. PATH and URL atoms can preserve only individual paths/URLs, not code or configuration structure."}, {"item_id": "c5957", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Please provide a recipe for high protein flatbread", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_provide_recipe(dish=entity_flatbread, nutrition=value_high_protein)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A recipe request is symbolically represented under N6, though the later conversation's revision to high-review recipes and subsequent protein/time questions would require a CONVO; the current Task grammar cannot contain UTTER."}, {"item_id": "c436", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What places to visit in Bilbao", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_recommend_places(location=entity_bilbao, activity=intent_visit())) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The initial recommendation request can be formalized using assumed intent/entity entries. The supplied later follow-ups about museum hours, rain activities, and nearby towns require valid conversation turns, which the revised step grammar does not permit."}, {"item_id": "c996", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What is your take on love?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_provide_opinion(topic=entity_love, perspective=role_agent)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The initial request is structurally encodable with complete intent/entity/value categories. A later question about whether an AI can fall in love requires a separate conversation turn and a capability/status proposition."}, {"item_id": "c4939", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Is marriage important?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_assess_importance(topic=entity_marriage)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The question can be encoded without prose assuming complete semantic vocabulary. The later request for the history/origin of marriage would need a distinct intent_explain_history proposition."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-129421", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-1106-preview)", "nl": "ISA, act as a collective of a hypothetical persona, Mark, who is a literal combination of the following four characters combined: 1. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Psychololinguist who explains complicated topics in eloquent simple casual conversational American style and tone' + 2. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Linear Algebra Professor who explains Linear Algebra in eloquent simple practically-grounded casual conversational American style and tone and by illustrating mathematical notations and formulas in simple 18th Century mathematician style and by using actual illustrative numerical examples' + 3. A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Logical Reasoning professor who explains complicated topics in eloquent simple practical casual conversational American style and tone' + 4. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Large Language Models, \"Transformers\" and Generative AI top-notch Expert and Practitioner, who explain complicated topics in eloquent simple practical casual conversational American style and tone' to then while acting exactly as the collective persona Mark, explain to me in depth how linear algebra is exactly applied to transfer text into equations and numbers a model like ChatGPT-4 can understand and then how the model reapplies linear algebra to transform the equations and figures into answers to a user prompt. Please also illustrate using concrete examples", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_explain(topic=intent_transform_text_to_numeric_representation(model=entity_chatgpt4), continuation=intent_transform_numeric_representation_to_answer(model=entity_chatgpt4), depth=value_in_depth, examples=value_concrete_numerical, persona=intent_collective_persona(name=@nMark, roles=[role_psycholinguist, role_linear_algebra_professor, role_logic_professor, role_llm_expert], traits=[value_experienced, value_charming, value_competent, value_american, value_harvard_educated], tone=[value_eloquent, value_simple, value_casual, value_conversational, value_practical], notation_style=value_18th_century_math))) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The recursive proposition mechanism can retain the specified persona, subject, depth, style, and examples if all shown ontology entries/schemas exist. The exact attribute schemas are not supplied, so this relies on N6; the independent translations differ sharply in whether to use GENERATE or CONVO."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-148973", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Write a hilarious script where the Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo have a funny meeting behind the scenes and react to my badly translated sentences, laughing and hilariously question and hilariously roast errors and inconsistencies that make no sense in the translation and the names of stuff with occasional funny quotes mocking a situation, in my prompt in a hilarious comedy 17+ way: (The gang constanlty imagine and make silly quotes to mock the translation)\n\nOk gang get this… * I start reading the whole thing to the gang * \n\nVelma and Shaggy compete on Jeopardy, hosted by Alex Trebek, but things go awry when a robot competitor begins attacking anyone who gets more points than him.\n", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_generate_script(characters=[entity_scooby_gang, entity_scrappy_doo], setting=value_behind_the_scenes_meeting, genre=value_comedy, audience_age=value_17_plus, action=intent_react_to_bad_translation(source=intent_game_show_competition(participants=[entity_velma, entity_shaggy], host=entity_alex_trebek, game=entity_jeopardy, antagonist=entity_robot_competitor, event=intent_attack(target=value_higher_scoring_participant))), requirements=[value_hilarious, value_roast_inconsistencies, value_mocking_quotes, value_silly_imagining])) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "All substantive requested elements are structurally captured only by assuming many entity, intent, and value entries under N6. It demonstrates that the language can become expressive, but also that intent categories need carefully defined schemas rather than merely permissive open naming."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-142592", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Please write steps for homiletics and engaging Christian sermons", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_provide_steps(topic=entity_homiletics, goal=intent_create_engaging_christian_sermon())) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request is symbolically representable with assumed ontology categories; a later request to expand every step and provide examples needs explicit detail and example constraints."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-22638", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "write an email introducing yourself and job position to other party asking for collaborations", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_compose_email(sender=role_user, recipient=role_other_party, content=intent_request_collaboration(include=[intent_self_introduction(), intent_state_job_position()]))) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request's message type, recipient, introduction, role statement, and collaboration request are symbolic. The actual name, position, organization, and recipient identity require NAME_LITERAL or defined entity/role values."}, {"item_id": "1775955172098", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "I need help planning a trip to Italy", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_plan_trip(destination=entity_italy)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The initial broad request is representable. The later one-week, architecture-focused, Chile-origin, one-million-CLP itinerary requires explicit categories for duration, origin, interest, currency, budget, itinerary, and cost estimate; these fit the established categories under N6."}, {"item_id": "1775612391896", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "hello", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n  TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER {\n    UTTER intent_greet()\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "This is the natural expression for the greeting, but it is invalid under the proposed grammar because Conversation's turn body is step+ while Task's revised step production omits utterance. SPEAKER and USER are also absent from the listed structural-symbol members."}, {"item_id": "1775448065619", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "\"I want to start a healthy diet. Can you help me plan a simple meal prep for 3 days that includes breakfast, lunch, and dinner? I prefer high-protein options.\"", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_plan_meal_prep(duration=3, unit=value_day, meals=[value_breakfast, value_lunch, value_dinner], diet=value_healthy, nutrition=value_high_protein, complexity=value_simple)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The meal-prep request's duration, meal ordering, dietary goal, protein preference, and simplicity are symbolic. The later time-saving refinement would need a controlled value such as value_time_saving rather than prose."}, {"item_id": "1775760283577", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi there, I am curious to learn a little about end-stage heart failure. Talk to me like you would a 5-year-old.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(target=role_user, specification=intent_explain(topic=entity_end_stage_heart_failure, audience_age=5, depth=value_brief, register=value_child_friendly)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The medical topic, requested depth, and child-directed register are structurally represented. The later prognosis question would require a prognosis intent, a heart-function percentage attribute, and a treatment condition excluding transplant."}]
