  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Task",
   "grammar": "document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (\":\" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= HANDLE \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= RETURN value\nstep ::= action | utterance | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation\ntask_call ::= HANDLE arg_list? (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value",
   "semantics": "A document has one ENTRYPOINT naming a declared Task or Conversation by canonical HANDLE. TASK ANCHOR is optional only when the source request contains no relative temporal expression. When a source contains relative time, its enclosing Task must carry exactly one ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL; translation resolves the relative expression once against that anchor and emits the resulting DATE_LITERAL in the relevant schema attribute. The anchor remains auditable source data and is not read at runtime. `step` includes UTTER so Turn bodies are grammatically valid; however, UTTER is statically illegal in a TASK body and legal only in a CONVO TURN body. All declarations, parameters, results, arguments, and calls use canonical handles or schema-authorized symbols.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "If the manager has not replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up.",
    "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v2 ANCHOR @d2026-09-30 {\n  ACTION op_check_replied(attr_target=role_manager, attr_deadline=@d2026-10-02) -> $v3:BOOL\n  IF NOT $v3 THEN { ACTION op_send_email(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_content=int_follow_up(attr_tone=val_polite)) }\n}"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Task is an ordered executable procedure with canonical opaque handles. Its optional date anchor makes relative-date translation reproducible and auditable.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "This restores valid Turn use of UTTER and replaces lost temporal auditability with a typed, closed translation anchor.",
   "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)",
   "addresses_note": "N1"
  },
  {
   "op": "revise",
   "construct_name": "Conversation",
   "grammar": "conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER \"=\" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT",
   "semantics": "Conversation is an ordered symbolic dialogue. TURN bodies use Task's shared step production, including UTTER, but the placement rule permits UTTER only here. TURN handles follow global preorder allocation. REPLY_TO and REVISES must name earlier turn handles in the same conversation. Revision remains whole-body replacement along a single tip-only chain. Every utterance uses an intent schema and contains no prose payload.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "The user asks for two days, then corrects the request to three days.",
